{:db/id 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186175737 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186176418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4b68033b-a4e7-5f65-b638-4450f1d5a103" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186174741}] :question/id #uuid "d3eb7ad1-efad-5cfc-9def-a4730d00ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214038" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 5)$ and $(7, 3)$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id 17592186176429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6f703b-eaf6-555f-839c-9af87138f4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{5}x + 5" :db/id 17592186174755} {:answer/id #uuid "aff9fd7a-670e-5495-8397-7ce141521dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{6}{5}x + 5" :db/id 17592186174756} {:answer/id #uuid "3af724d4-8486-55c0-80a0-48cfab90e646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{6}{5}x + 5" :db/id 17592186174757} {:answer/id #uuid "5014213f-3b1a-5ad8-8f00-3c24163d61cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5}{6}x + 5" :db/id 17592186174758} {:answer/id #uuid "647e66bd-ab6f-558e-92de-5849d1064776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{5}{6}x + 5" :db/id 17592186174759}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af060219-3ae1-5711-9156-cac1c55d3e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186174754}] :question/id #uuid "42d56d4c-2843-5563-82a0-0618b673d148" :question/math-academy-id "q-22642" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0, 5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(3, 2)$."} {:db/id 17592186235495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c9ed58-392f-5223-bc99-c5d9072bf047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x-6" :db/id 17592186234851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c9ed58-392f-5223-bc99-c5d9072bf047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x-6" :db/id 17592186234851} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c93770-8bbc-5afb-aed5-b4b4b6387392" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186235197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2753316-4b68-5a78-b169-53e3c0eb33f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-167797" :question/problem "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-3,$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-5).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{1}{3}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186238007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35533677-024f-5015-b5fc-c9c35bcd97a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id 17592186236155} {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id 17592186236269} {:answer/id #uuid "8c418cf2-df35-513d-86a0-953d17273ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+8" :db/id 17592186236703} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d730c-740a-5089-a348-d56552dfbfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id 17592186237047} {:answer/id #uuid "f31cf18d-2ca6-52a8-ae8f-44129d24abf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id 17592186237350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id 17592186236269} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae135409-b9c1-54f7-b3eb-0d0be03cf77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758e4600-77be-53f9-a21f-0c9a0a5da17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103440" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{-4-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(2,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186238010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f907072-63f6-5f5f-8f48-b581ee8d9964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+9" :db/id 17592186236017} {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id 17592186236471} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbc543c-735d-56bd-91ca-9de8c441f245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+19" :db/id 17592186236814} {:answer/id #uuid "a80fa7f7-5d9d-58db-96e1-7ccc0ab7f96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}x-9" :db/id 17592186236869} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f63932-6678-5b50-ad61-c13da4cdb606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+9" :db/id 17592186236977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id 17592186236471} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87be670-f763-5d7b-8ef2-17d4e48d4561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4caa1a7-6407-5b10-be10-2977d4647c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103455" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(8,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57533672-f7c9-578c-bd03-fcbcba3dcbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id 17592186288638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57533672-f7c9-578c-bd03-fcbcba3dcbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id 17592186288638} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a84cf4d-a7d6-504e-983d-f05875b4e775" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55729d30-da9a-5863-9116-cd05f93a8d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-215768" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-4 & =x \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1024e4c0-bbcd-5e61-84f8-3dff5e50f5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}x-3" :db/id 17592186288154} {:answer/id #uuid "297e02ff-5dc0-5fd3-8271-aee2c38fccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3" :db/id 17592186288308} {:answer/id #uuid "6a24547b-b78e-5d0e-90b1-33cec5f86ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-3" :db/id 17592186288781} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fb0467-1a97-512a-aa0f-150efb576a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-3" :db/id 17592186289326} {:answer/id #uuid "c4988bec-b2bd-5011-9ca7-75d333c159db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x-3" :db/id 17592186289332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "297e02ff-5dc0-5fd3-8271-aee2c38fccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3" :db/id 17592186288308} :answer-field/id #uuid "c865f6fa-cf35-513a-a3d6-3e960c11b6b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6cef704-dc0c-5ab7-9bc9-e3a20f725642" :question/math-academy-id "q-23453" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-7}{-5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e701b8f4-7d6b-5c45-b082-b976895be196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id 17592186289564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e701b8f4-7d6b-5c45-b082-b976895be196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id 17592186289564} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6596ac-82a2-591c-bfcf-0cf47b94c5ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186290207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c855e16-f691-5fbd-bcf1-319b489f96c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-237036" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-5,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-5,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x+5) \\\\ y-5 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "701eb222-0215-5e1e-aeed-f86ef2b1e8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id 17592186288811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "701eb222-0215-5e1e-aeed-f86ef2b1e8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id 17592186288811} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e36efb-7123-5c18-8518-75c3551a7b86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7172ccc4-2a2a-5ae6-a89d-55424e3bf138" :question/math-academy-id "q-237224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2deda78d-e190-5ac6-8f09-dfdafe1448fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}x-5" :db/id 17592186288341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2deda78d-e190-5ac6-8f09-dfdafe1448fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}x-5" :db/id 17592186288341} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb2bc39-aed8-54cc-8f5c-a5e009a5e97a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01d34255-de74-5cbb-862e-52884bec6cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-237225" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-3)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =-\\frac{2}{7}(x-0) \\\\ y+5 & =-\\frac{2}{7}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186339134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a66ab68-36c3-5c49-97a3-eba1003dd886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x-13" :db/id 17592186336128} {:answer/id #uuid "32cee572-2261-55af-9018-9c7a204018f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+6" :db/id 17592186336514} {:answer/id #uuid "59ad4d32-5750-5654-afec-1553cc4b52bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id 17592186336851} {:answer/id #uuid "972da17b-34f0-585d-920b-18729c1e5078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{6}x+4" :db/id 17592186337408} {:answer/id #uuid "dec392c4-3ead-52cd-a574-74279c757e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x-3" :db/id 17592186337974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ad4d32-5750-5654-afec-1553cc4b52bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id 17592186336851} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e485dbb-42bf-52c7-84d1-8c3ba7c006ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91f3a022-c4bb-5e42-8f8c-ad041616b931" :question/math-academy-id "q-72567" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-6,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-6}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{2} \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-6)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-6,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-(-6)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+6) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186341834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f3e7e-a692-5e84-84d3-bb04895e42c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/id 17592186339750} {:answer/id #uuid "7de51bcc-dba4-5f66-9ed1-e89e2285349b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id 17592186340264} {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a70a6-b427-555b-b378-aaf49435f770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7" :db/id 17592186341084} {:answer/id #uuid "f252e1db-2d4f-5cfe-b195-696e6f606c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/id 17592186341262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id 17592186340623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e67ea3-0bdd-589c-834c-8bb372afcf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99fe5e6a-20b3-53d0-a013-c1c2c0d3ad0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72568" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}
